Lab 16: Scoped Service Access Configuration
Objectives
Understand and implement scoped token authentication in OpenStack
Configure role-based access control (RBAC) using Keystone policies
Validate service access restrictions through scoped tokens
Demonstrate domain and project-level access control
Prerequisites
OpenStack cloud environment (DevStack or production)
OpenStack CLI tools installed (python-openstackclient)
Admin credentials for Keystone
Basic understanding of OpenStack identity concepts
Access to a Linux terminal
Setup Requirements
Install OpenStack client:
pip install python-openstackclient
Source your OpenStack RC file:
source admin-openrc.sh
Task 1: Configure Scoped Tokens
Subtask 1.1: Create Domain-Scoped Token
List available domains:

openstack domain list
Expected Output: Table showing available domains

Request domain-scoped token:

openstack token issue --domain <domain_name>
Explanation: This creates a token limited to operations within the specified domain

Verify token scope:

openstack token show <token_id> -c domain -c roles
Troubleshooting: If denied, ensure your admin user has sufficient privileges

Subtask 1.2: Create Project-Scoped Token
List projects:

openstack project list
Request project-scoped token:

openstack token issue --project <project_name>
Test access:

openstack server list
Expected Outcome: Only shows instances within the scoped project

Task 2: Implement Keystone Policies
Subtask 2.1: Modify Policy.json
Locate Keystone policy file:

sudo vi /etc/keystone/policy.json
Add custom rule (example):

{
  "identity:get_user": "role:admin or (role:user and domain_id:%(target.user.domain_id)s)"
}
Key Concept: This restricts user access to their own domain

Apply changes:

sudo systemctl restart apache2
Subtask 2.2: Test Policy Enforcement
As domain user, attempt to list all users:
openstack user list
Expected Outcome: Access denied for non-admin users
Task 3: Validate Access Control
Subtask 3.1: Role-Based Verification
Create test role:

openstack role create observer
Assign role to user:

openstack role add --user <user> --project <project> observer
Test restricted access:

openstack image create --disk-format qcow2 test_image
Expected Outcome: Operation fails due to insufficient permissions

Subtask 3.2: Cross-Domain Testing
Attempt cross-domain operation with domain token:
openstack user list --domain <other_domain>
Expected Outcome: Access denied for foreign domain operations
Conclusion
This lab demonstrated:

Successful implementation of domain and project-scoped tokens
Configuration of granular access control through Keystone policies
Validation of role-based restrictions across OpenStack services
Key Takeaways:

Scoped tokens provide security boundaries in multi-tenant environments
Keystone policies enable fine-grained access control
Proper testing is essential to verify access restrictions
Next Steps
